COPY public.global_knowledge (id, knowledge_type, domain, topic, title, summary, full_content, key_points, source_organization, source_url, evidence_level, publication_date, applies_to_demographics, contraindications, times_referenced, relevance_score, created_at, updated_at, is_active) FROM stdin;
1	guideline	nephrology	dialysis_adequacy	KDOQI: Hemodialysis Adequacy Minimum Targets	Single-pool KtV (spKtV) ≥1.2 per session is the minimum target (KDOQI). URR ≥65% is the equivalent target. KtV <1.0 = severely inadequate dialysis. DOPPS data: each 0.1 rise in KtV is associated with a ~7% reduction in mortality.	\N	["spKtV \\u22651.2 minimum; URR \\u226565% equivalent", "KtV <1.0 = severely inadequate, urgent review needed", "Target KtV 1.4\\u20131.5 in practice for safety margin", "DOPPS: 7% mortality reduction per 0.1 KtV increase"]	KDOQI / DOPPS	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.520121+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.520128+00	t
2	guideline	nephrology	phosphorus_management	KDIGO 2017: Phosphorus Target for Dialysis Patients	Target serum phosphorus <5.5 mg/dL (KDIGO 2017). Values >5.5 mg/dL drive secondary hyperparathyroidism and vascular calcification. Values >7.0 mg/dL are crisis level. Both dietary restriction (<1,000 mg/day) and phosphate binders taken WITH meals are required.	\N	["Target: <5.5 mg/dL", ">5.5 = elevated; >7.0 = crisis \\u2014 urgent review", "Binders (Sevelamer, CaCO\\u2083) must be taken at the START of each meal", "Additive phosphates in processed foods (E450\\u2013452) are most bioavailable \\u2014 read labels"]	KDIGO CKD-MBD 2017	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.527086+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.527089+00	t
3	guideline	nephrology	potassium_management	Hyperkalemia Critical Thresholds in ESRD	Pre-dialysis K⁺ target: 3.5–5.5 mEq/L. K⁺ >5.5 mEq/L = hyperkalemia, arrhythmia risk — urgent dietary review. K⁺ >6.5 mEq/L = EMERGENCY, immediate contact with care team. Dietary restriction: <2,000–3,000 mg/day of potassium.	\N	["Target K\\u207a: 3.5\\u20135.5 mEq/L", ">5.5 = URGENT (arrhythmia risk)", ">6.5 = EMERGENCY \\u2014 call care team immediately", "Restrict: bananas, oranges, tomatoes, potatoes, avocado, beans, dairy, salt substitutes (KCl)", "Dietary K\\u207a limit: 2,000\\u20133,000 mg/day"]	KDOQI / KDIGO	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.530892+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.530896+00	t
4	guideline	nephrology	pth_bone_disease	KDIGO CKD-MBD: PTH Target and Bone Disease	Intact PTH (iPTH) target for dialysis patients: 150–600 pg/mL (KDIGO 2017). iPTH <150 = adynamic bone disease (fracture risk, bone pain). iPTH >600 = secondary hyperparathyroidism (bone resorption, vascular calcification). Calcium target: 8.4–9.5 mg/dL. Phosphorus and active Vitamin D directly control PTH secretion.	\N	["iPTH target: 150\\u2013600 pg/mL", "<150 = adynamic bone disease (oversuppressed)", ">600 = secondary hyperparathyroidism", "Ca target: 8.4\\u20139.5 mg/dL", "Treat high phosphorus first \\u2014 it drives secondary HPT"]	KDIGO CKD-MBD 2017	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.534328+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.534331+00	t
5	guideline	nephrology	anemia_management	KDIGO 2012: Anemia of CKD — Hemoglobin and Iron Targets	Hemoglobin target for dialysis patients: 10–11.5 g/dL. Avoid Hb >13 g/dL (thrombosis risk). Ferritin >200 µg/L and TSAT >20% are required before starting ESA therapy. IV iron is preferred over oral iron in HD patients (oral iron is poorly absorbed due to uremia). Hb <8 g/dL = severe anemia, exercise is contraindicated.	\N	["Hb target: 10\\u201311.5 g/dL", "Hb >13: avoid (increased thrombosis/stroke risk with ESA)", "Ferritin <200 \\u00b5g/L or TSAT <20% = iron deficient", "IV iron preferred in HD patients", "Hb <8 g/dL = contraindication to aerobic exercise"]	KDIGO Anemia 2012	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.538768+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.538771+00	t
6	guideline	nephrology	albumin_nutritional_status	KDOQI 2020: Albumin as Nutritional Marker in ESRD	Serum albumin <3.5 g/dL = significant malnutrition. Albumin <3.0 g/dL = severe malnutrition, strong mortality predictor. Target albumin: >4.0 g/dL. HD patients need 1.2 g/kg/day protein (NOT the 0.6–0.8g/kg of pre-dialysis CKD — dialysis is catabolic and dialysate removes amino acids).	\N	["Albumin target: >4.0 g/dL", "<3.5 = malnutrition; <3.0 = severe malnutrition", "HD protein needs: 1.2\\u20131.4 g/kg/day (higher than pre-dialysis CKD)", "Low albumin predicts hospitalisation and mortality"]	KDOQI Nutrition 2020	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.545002+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.545011+00	t
7	guideline	renal_nutrition	potassium_restricted_diet	Renal Diet: Dietary Potassium Restriction for HD Patients	HD patients should limit potassium to 2,000–3,000 mg/day. Highest-K foods: bananas (422mg), avocado (708mg), potato (897mg), sweet potato (540mg), spinach (839mg raw), beans (717mg/cup). Leaching vegetables (boil in large water, discard water) reduces potassium by 50–70%. Salt substitutes contain KCl — completely avoid.	\N	["Limit: 2,000\\u20133,000 mg/day", "Leaching reduces K by 50\\u201370% (boil, discard water, rinse)", "Salt substitutes = KCl \\u2014 dangerous for ESRD patients", "Potassium accumulates between sessions \\u2014 spread intake across the day", "Frozen vegetables have less K than canned (canning leaches K into brine, which is then discarded)"]	NKF / Renal Dietitians Australasia	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.550252+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.550258+00	t
8	guideline	renal_nutrition	phosphorus_restricted_diet	Renal Diet: Phosphorus Restriction and Phosphate Binder Timing	Limit phosphorus to 800–1,000 mg/day. Worst offenders: dairy products, cola drinks, processed meats containing additive phosphates (listed as E450, E451, E452 in ingredients — most bioavailable form), whole grains, nuts, seeds, beer. Phosphate binders MUST be taken at the START of each meal (first bite), not before or after.	\N	["Limit: 800\\u20131,000 mg/day", "Additive phosphates (E450\\u2013452 in processed foods) = 100% absorbed vs 40\\u201360% from natural sources", "Sevelamer preferred (no calcium/aluminium load)", "Binders with meals only \\u2014 not fasting or post-meal", "Vegetable protein (plant-based) has less bioavailable phosphorus than animal protein"]	KDOQI Nutrition / Journal of Renal Nutrition	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.553105+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.553107+00	t
9	guideline	renal_nutrition	fluid_restriction	Fluid Management: Interdialytic Weight Gain Targets	Target interdialytic weight gain: <1.5 kg/day, <3 kg/session. Weight gain >3 kg/session is associated with haemodynamic instability during dialysis. Total fluid intake = urine output + 500–750 mL (insensible losses). Reducing sodium intake (<2g/day) reduces thirst and is the most effective intervention for fluid control.	\N	["Target: <1.5 kg/day, <3 kg/session", ">3 kg: high risk for intradialytic hypotension", "Fluid = all liquids: soup, ice cream, gelatin, fruit juices", "Low sodium (<2g/day) is the most effective thirst/fluid management strategy", "Track daily weight same time each morning"]	KDOQI 2015	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.555296+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.555297+00	t
10	guideline	cardiology	bp_target_dialysis	KDIGO 2021: Blood Pressure Targets in HD Patients	Pre-dialysis BP target: <140/90 mmHg. Post-dialysis: <130/80 mmHg. Interdialytic hypertension (BP rises between sessions) is linked to sodium/fluid excess. ESRD patients have 10–20× higher cardiovascular mortality than the general population. ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce LV hypertrophy and mortality independent of BP effect.	\N	["Pre-HD BP target: <140/90 mmHg", "Post-HD target: <130/80 mmHg", "ESRD cardiovascular mortality: 10\\u201320\\u00d7 general population", "ACE-I/ARBs reduce LVH independently of BP (use them)", "Home BP self-monitoring preferred \\u2014 clinic BP is unreliable in HD patients"]	KDIGO BP 2021 / AHA	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.557156+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.557157+00	t
11	research_finding	cardiology	lv_hypertrophy_esrd	Left Ventricular Hypertrophy: Prevalence and Drivers in ESRD	LVH is present in ~75% of dialysis patients at initiation and is the strongest single predictor of cardiac death in ESRD. Key drivers: hypertension (pressure overload), fluid overload (volume overload → increased preload), anaemia (high-output state), AV fistula (left-to-right shunt contributing to LV dilation). Treating all four causes regresses LVH.	\N	["LVH prevalence in ESRD: ~75% at dialysis start", "LVH = strongest individual predictor of CV death in ESRD", "Drivers: HTN + fluid + anaemia + AVF (all four must be addressed)", "Every 10 mmHg BP reduction \\u2192 significant LVH regression", "Correcting anaemia (Hb to 10\\u201311.5) reduces cardiac output burden"]	USRDS / DOPPS / Zoccali et al. NEJM	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.559908+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.559909+00	t
12	research_finding	mental_health	depression_dialysis	Depression in Dialysis Patients: Prevalence and Impact	Depression affects 25–40% of dialysis patients — 5× higher than the general population. Depressed dialysis patients have 1.5–2× higher mortality risk. Depression strongly predicts non-adherence to dialysis schedule, dietary restrictions, and medications. PHQ-9 ≥10 = moderate depression. CBT is first-line before pharmacotherapy.	\N	["Prevalence: 25\\u201340% in dialysis vs ~8% general population", "PHQ-9 \\u226510 = moderate depression requiring clinical attention", "Depression \\u2192 1.5\\u20132\\u00d7 mortality increase", "Non-adherence is often depression as root cause \\u2014 not wilfulness", "CBT and behavioural activation are first-line treatments"]	KDQOL / DOPPS / APA	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.562122+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.562122+00	t
13	best_practice	mental_health	coping_strategies_esrd	Evidence-Based Coping Strategies for ESRD and Chronic Illness	Most effective psychological interventions for ESRD: 1) Behavioural Activation — scheduling enjoyable activities despite fatigue; 2) Cognitive Restructuring — challenging catastrophising thoughts; 3) Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) — accept illness, commit to valued life; 4) Peer support — HD patients benefit significantly from kidney disease community; 5) Meaning-Making — finding purpose beyond the illness.	\N	["Behavioural activation: 1\\u20132 enjoyable activities/week regardless of energy level", "Social support: 50% lower depression risk with strong social ties", "Sleep hygiene: 60\\u201380% of ESRD patients have sleep disorders (RLS, apnea)", "Fatigue pacing: plan activities for the day after dialysis \\u2014 not the dialysis day", "Peer support groups are the most effective non-pharmacological intervention"]	APA / Cochrane / KDQOL	\N	moderate	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.564508+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.564509+00	t
14	guideline	exercise_physiology	intradialytic_exercise	Cochrane 2022: Intradialytic Exercise Safety and Efficacy	Exercise DURING the first 2 hours of a 4-hour HD session is safe and beneficial. Benefits: improves KtV by 5–10% (enhanced solute mobilisation from tissue compartments), reduces post-dialysis fatigue by ~30%, improves cardiovascular fitness, muscle strength. Stationary cycling on the dialysis chair is the most practical modality. Contraindications: systolic BP <90 pre-exercise, recent vascular access surgery (<6 weeks).	\N	["Safe timing: first 2 hours of HD session only", "Improves KtV by 5\\u201310% (better toxin clearance)", "Reduces post-dialysis fatigue by ~30%", "Best modality: stationary bike on the dialysis chair", "Avoid: exercise on the fistula arm; stop if BP drops >20 mmHg"]	Cochrane Systematic Review 2022 / DOPPS	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.56662+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.566621+00	t
15	guideline	exercise_physiology	exercise_safety_esrd	ACSM: Exercise Safety Guidelines for ESRD Patients	Absolute contraindications: uncontrolled hypertension (>180/110 mmHg), severe anaemia (Hb <8 g/dL), recent cardiac event (<6 weeks), active vascular access infection. The fistula arm must NOT be used for heavy resistance exercise, blood pressure measurement, or IV access. Recommended intensity: RPE 12–14 (moderate). Non-dialysis days are preferred for higher-intensity sessions than HD days.	\N	["Stop if BP >180/110 or if systolic drops >20 mmHg during exercise", "Hb <8 g/dL = defer exercise until anaemia is treated", "NEVER stress the fistula arm", "RPE 12\\u201314 (moderate) is the safe zone", "Non-dialysis days preferred for higher-intensity work"]	ACSM / Renal Rehabilitation Guidelines	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.568438+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.568439+00	t
16	guideline	dialysis_nursing	vascular_access_care	KDOQI 2019: AV Fistula Self-Care and Monitoring	AV fistula (AVF) is gold-standard access. Daily self-monitoring: feel the thrill (vibration) and listen for the bruit (humming sound) every morning. Change in thrill/bruit = possible stenosis — notify the dialysis team immediately. NEVER allow BP cuff, blood draw, or IV line in the fistula arm. Avoid constrictive clothing, watches, sleeping on the fistula arm. AVF takes 3–6 months to mature; early access is the most common cause of failure.	\N	["Daily check: feel thrill (vibration), listen for bruit (hum)", "Loss of thrill/bruit = call dialysis centre immediately", "NEVER: BP cuff, blood draw, or IV in the fistula arm", "NEVER: sleep on fistula arm or wear tight watches", "Maturation: 3\\u20136 months before first cannulation"]	KDOQI Vascular Access 2019	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.569968+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.569969+00	t
17	best_practice	dialysis_nursing	interdialytic_monitoring	Interdialytic Weight Monitoring and Fluid Safety Alerts	Weigh every morning at the same time, same scale, after waking and voiding but before eating. Alert care team if: >2 kg gain in one day, shortness of breath at rest or lying flat, ankle/leg swelling, inability to lie flat (orthopnea). Post-dialysis hypotension (systolic <90 mmHg or drop >20 mmHg) is the most common HD complication — suggests fluid was removed too aggressively or dry weight needs adjustment.	\N	["Daily weight \\u2192 alert if >2 kg gain/day or >3 kg total since last session", "Alert: shortness of breath at rest or lying flat", "Alert: ankle/leg swelling that wasn't there before", "Post-HD hypotension: systolic <90 or >20 mmHg drop", "Cramps during HD = fluid removal too fast or electrolyte imbalance"]	ANNA Renal Nursing Standards 2023	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.572357+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.572358+00	t
18	best_practice	general_practice	polypharmacy_esrd	Medication Safety and Polypharmacy in ESRD	Dialysis patients take an average of 10–15 medications daily. Critical safety concerns: NSAIDs are CONTRAINDICATED in patients with residual renal function (accelerate CKD progression). ACE-I and ARBs raise serum potassium — titrate carefully. Some drugs are dialysed out and need supplemental post-HD dosing (certain antibiotics, some antiepileptics). QT-prolonging drugs are particularly dangerous with electrolyte abnormalities (K⁺, Mg²⁺).	\N	["NSAIDs: CONTRAINDICATED if residual renal function remains", "ACE-I/ARBs + high K\\u207a diet = dangerous hyperkalaemia combination", "Post-HD supplemental dosing: required for some antibiotics (vancomycin, acyclovir)", "QT prolongation risk: amplified by K\\u207a <3.5 or Mg\\u00b2\\u207a <0.8", "Medication reconciliation at every clinical visit"]	KDOQI / FDA Renal Dosing Database / BNF Renal	\N	high	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.575062+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.575063+00	t
19	research_finding	general_practice	sleep_esrd	Sleep Disorders in ESRD: 60–80% Prevalence	Sleep disorders affect 60–80% of dialysis patients. Most common: restless legs syndrome (RLS) — linked to iron deficiency and uraemia; obstructive sleep apnea (worsened by fluid overload causing upper airway oedema); insomnia. RLS improvement correlates strongly with correction of iron deficiency (ferritin, TSAT). Sleep deprivation worsens fatigue, depression, and dialysis tolerance.	\N	["Sleep disorders: 60\\u201380% in ESRD vs ~15% general population", "RLS: treat iron deficiency first (ferritin, TSAT)", "Fluid overload causes pharyngeal oedema \\u2192 worsens sleep apnea", "Dialysis schedule (Mon/Wed/Fri) fragments sleep patterns", "Poor sleep \\u2192 worse fatigue, lower mood, poorer dietary adherence"]	KDQOL / AASM / DOPPS Sleep Data	\N	moderate	\N	\N	\N	0	1	2026-04-20 14:49:37.577629+00	2026-04-20 14:49:37.57763+00	t
